The New Symphony Steering Committee, a service organization formed last year in Miami to spearhead the drive for a tax-supported regional orchestra in South Florida, voted recently to authorize its executive committee to negotiate an annual residency in Miami by the Pittsburgh Symphony.
According to committee chairman David Weaver, the move resulted from an analysis of the orchestral situation following the repeal of the occupational license tax increase that had been approved in Dade and Broward counties last summer.
"It was clear that we would get no government funding for an orchestra," Weaver said. "The idea of bringing the Pittsburgh Symphony to Miami was seen as an alternative, at least during an interim period for several years.
"The orchestra would come in for a five-month concert series, a week a month, between November and March. There would be two major concerts each of those weeks and also two other concerts, either pops or children's programs. The orchestra would also get involved in a range of community activities -- going into the schools, for example."
Weaver said members of the steering committee authorized the negotiations with the Pittsburgh Symphony "with the caveat that it had to result in a joint, sister-city relationship between Miami and Pittsburgh, which would either lead to a much broader relationship or act as an interim for developing a response to the need for a regional orchestra."
"The objective is to provide world-class symphonic music in the Miami area," added Steven Bronfenbrenner, director of finance and operations for the Greater Miami Opera and a member of the steering committee.
"Hopefully, this would stimulate the beginning of a regional orchestra audience and perhaps stimulate future interest and support in a local effort. It is clearly a short-term solution, and would be evaluated on an annual basis."
