Safety scandals are not deterring patients from seeking treatment at failing hospitals, a new study says.
Researchers from Imperial College London studied patient numbers at three hospitals that underwent highly critical and widely publicised failures reported by the Healthcare Commission, a predecessor of the Care Quality Commission.
Despite widespread media coverage of serious lapses in performance, they found that this had no overall effect on patient numbers at two of the hospitals, and only a temporary decline at the third.
The findings add to a growing body of research that question the role of patient choice to improve healthcare services, a central component of the health and social care bill.
Researchers looked at University hospitals of Leicester trust and Maidstone and Tunbridge Wells, both investigated for the poor management of Clostridium difficile in 2007, and Mid-Staffordshire foundation trust, investigated for a high number of death rates in emergency admissions in 2009.
The serious failings had no overall effect on patient numbers in Leicester and Mid-Staffordshire.
Maidstone and Tunbridge Wells had 12% fewer patients at three months and 14% fewer patients at six months following the publication of the investigation, compared with patient numbers at nearby hospitals, but figures returned to normal soon after the report came out.
In the study, published in the Health Affairs journal, the authors conclude: "Our finding that these highly publicised quality failings did not have a large or sustained impact on levels of health service use reinforces the view that simplistic assumptions regarding the power of information to drive patient choices are unrealistic."
