The coach of the team that won by a touchdown on the road put his hands in the air not in aggravation, but in concession. He even smiled a little, buoyed by a victory that was no sure thing from the bracing opening minutes, admitting his team had a way to go.
"Obviously," Nick Saban said, "we haven't put it all together yet."
Hours later, darkness descended on another coach after his own hair-raising road win and Brian Kelly similarly looked on the bright side for Notre Dame. In good cheer, he asserted his team was still a work in progress, searching for an identity. He repeated this in the light of day Sunday.
"Being on the road back-to-back against Michigan and Purdue, you're going to be in for tough games," Kelly said during a conference call the day after a 31-24 win over Purdue.
"We're still finding out who's the middle linebacker. Who's playing safety. Who's playing wide receiver. There's a number of positions we're still trying to find ourselves at. We're gaining an identity on offense with Tommy (Rees) at quarterback. I knew it was going to take us a little time to get the pieces together."
There is biding time, and just trying to buy it.
It is quite possibly rubbish to keep comparing the Irish and the Crimson Tide, but it's also the standard Notre Dame set for itself. So Saban's crew absorbed the wind burn from the propulsive Johnny Manziel, emerging intact. Kelly's club survived the nation's No. 116 offense and dropped a spot to No. 22 in the Associated Press poll.
The messages sound the same, but one is the soundtrack to a pursuit of perfection and one to a pursuit of answers.
Notre Dame knows what it wants to do and how it must play. There is no identity search there. The talk of "evolving" still after three games is an attempt to soften the reality that players will either start doing the job, or the year will be spent masking deficiencies that won't go away.
"There's a whole bunch of room for improvement," receiver DaVaris Daniels said. "We have good practices, but we have to show it on the field. We're a much better team than what we showed."
It's entirely possible that's true. Proof hasn't arrived yet.
