According to Rabbi Dovid Gutnick �� the first third-generation rabbi from that famous Melbourne Jewish family �� the challenge is to find a Judaism that appeals to modern Jews. Many synagogues were founded by postwar European immigrants who were very committed, he says. "Their children are not as committed but there's still a sentimental attachment. Their grandchildren are not coming to synagogue at all, except three times a year, and will likely marry out of the faith." He says his 150-year-old East Melbourne shule is a cross between the traditional synagogue and the boutique model. "We are down to less than 200 families, and it's started to become a battle to bring in younger families. So we have to make it a personal place to be, a friendly environment. We don't have a cantor (the singer who leads the prayers) or choir or a lot of formalities. "It's a kind of evolving for the Jewish community. We are going into a new era. It's the challenge for any religion to show that it's as relevant in the internet and iPod age as it was in the days of stetl (prewar East European Jewish village) and wagon." Shelly Freeman, who is preparing a documentary on the rise of the boutique shules, thinks that, by and large, they are meeting that challenge. "When I was growing up in the 1980s the only synagogues were St Kilda, Caulfield, East Melbourne, Mizrachi, South Caulfield, Kew, Doncaster, Elwood and Toorak. Suddenly there's a proliferation of boutique shules that have taken over, each with their own personality."
